d  omicron and maybe think well now we  don't need another booster that's quite  possible or they're waiting for that  three-month mark so we might see still  see some uptake  uh past you know three months on from  the peak of the  omicron wave that's possible  but i think  there's some misconceptions about um  you know some of the vaccine  effectiveness  um some people might think well you told  us two was enough and now you're saying  three and maybe even four  well i think our knowledge evolves and  we're giving the best advice based on  what we're seeing  and it is  so important for you to keep following  uh that advice and then of course there  is a group of individuals who may not  want to get vaccinated  we have other vaccine options  being made more available  as it stands this week we got our first  delivery of novavax vaccine  and given this uptick in cases  i would  encourage anyone who hasn't been  vaccinated particularly the ones who  haven't even had the primary series to  take advantage of this opportunity i  think in the next days and the next  couple of weeks you'll see the provinces  being able to deploy that  so all of these things might help  um but i think um you know don't don't  be complacent in thinking this is over  even if the wave is slightly smaller  you can do everything that you can  do  in terms of boosters and masking to help  us get back to all the things that we  all want to do  [Music]  uh  um  um  next question erica ibrahim from  canadian press  hi there um  i uh i heard some of my francophone  colleagues  pose this question and i was hoping to  pose it in english  dr tam what do you think of the general  trend of jurisdictions relaxing uh  restrictions at a time um  uh when case counts seem to be on the  rise is now the time to be easing  restrictions and um as a i guess a  follow-up is easing up responsible for  um the the modeling  sure the increase in the activity in a  way is totally expected because any time  we relax  some of these measures and increase  in-person activities or more contacts  cases are going to go up  and that everyone knew about that  on top of that  there's the ba2 variant which  based on the information we have  internationally is more transmissible  and that particularly is going to affect  people who haven't  been up to date with a vaccination or  haven't had that ba1  wave as much in their community and  waning immunity may have occurred in  some populations so all these things  alone it's not just  did someone relax mask mandates that is  not as a singular  you know decision  would be responsible for resurgence it's  a combination i think of all those  things but any time we relax measures  cases go up so the key is whether we can  keep those hospitalizations and severity  trends down but keeping transmission  lower  we can all do because that protects um  the the persons who are at the higher  risk  as well as keeping that uh population  impact lower  um so so i think  we can't see every uptake as being  um you know a massive issue  but we have to still keep at it in terms  of uh trying to dampen that wave as much  as we can  uh while our boosters are still  um you know there's some gaps in our  booster coverage  uh but but you know this is a very  different situation than we were and  it's still going to be very difficult  because balancing transmission and  reducing transmission  and  the reducing severity against the need  for society to return to more normal  stance it's going to be quite a  difficult balance and that's why i think  people have to watch that every  you know very much on an ongoing basis  but as individuals we can still keep up  with those measures  and go get your booster  okay erica did you have a second  question  uh no it was a bit of a double-barreled  question so that was my i rolled my  follow in there too thank you okay yes  and does anyone else want to ask a  question  if so please use the raised hand  function  and seeing no rais